From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Sun, 1 May 1994 17:12:14 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-5-9] PCR: Objectivist criticisms of PCR

On Sat, 30 Apr 1994, Tim Starr wrote:

> Then can we agree that infallible knowledge is impossible and irrelevant to
> the question of direct awareness, drop the subject of infallibility, and get
> on to the question of why you object to the radical realist theory of
> perception?

I don't really object to it; I simply consider the differences between it 
and representationalism to be indifferent with regard to epistemological 
problems. I don't know whether the radical realist theory of perception 
is true or false, but I'm quite sure it's irrelevant to an explanation of 
knowledge.

> The notion that we don't perceive causation is a Humean position that is
> rejected by Objectivists like Kelley.  The Objectivist position is that we do,
> in fact, perceive causation.  Either in his book or in some lectures he taped
> he details how he thinks this happens.  If it's in his book, I think it's in
> the latter part of it ("Foundations and Penumbras," I think it's called), not
> the first part.

Kelley's discussions of direct perception of causation seem frankly 
mystical to me, as do his discussions of "nonpropositional justification" 
of propositions. I am trying, but I have yet to render any sense from them.

> Kant's Critique of Pure Reason was in reply to Hume's skeptical arguments
> against Cartesian empiricism.  That's not "lumping in," that's historical fact.

This historical fact does not make Kelley's rebuttals of Descartes and 
Kant any more applicable to Hume's arguments.

> Then we agree that Baconian, Cartesian-empiricist induction is impossible.  I
> disagree that this exhausts the class of theories of induction, however, and
> would maintain that the Aristotelian-Objectivist theory holds true.

Well, since the Objectivist theory of induction seems to be grounded in 
nonpropositional justification of propositions, which looks like a 
magical process to me, I'm afraid I just don't understand it yet.

> >I thought Max's posts on the topic were a lot better than mine. What 
> >questions were unanswered?
> 
> The ones about the political disanalogies and why he thinks that Kelley's
> position shares the failings of Cartesian empiricism.

Well, I now understand that Kelley's position does not share the failings 
of Cartesian empiricism, but goes on to invent new failings of its own. 
As for the political disanalogies, I think they were merely illustrative, 
but I'm not sure which ones you are referring to.

> All I need is a rule which tells me which kinds of criticisms are accepted
> and which ones aren't.  Thus far, all I've gotten is something like: all
> cards are wild except for justificationist ones.  And I don't recall ever
> having gotten a good definition of justificationism.

It is an unfortunate fact of life that the universe has yet to hand us a 
simply-stated rule for such situations. Perhaps we will discover one 
someday. The acquisition of knowledge is not easy (many Objectivists seem 
to think otherwise); the only reason we have gotten as far as we have is 
because of cultural accumulation of knowledge over the centuries.

The proper "ranking" of criticisms is contextually dependent on the
problem under consideration. I don't like this messy fact any better than
you do, but fact it remains. Empirical criteria are not very useful
criteria by which to judge a theorem of tensor calculus, but in other
problems, empirical criteria are far and away the most useful. 
Formulating a taxonomy of knowledge-problems, and the appropriate
corresponding criticism-rankings, is an interesting research program for
pancritical rationalists. 

As far as the definition of justification in this context goes,
justification is logical deduction. "Axiomatic reasoning" if you like. I'm
sure you're familiar with the process of justifying a theorem by logical
deduction from premises. The quest for ultimate justification, however,
leads either to infinite regress (as we deduce the premises from other
premises), or to magical processes such as Kelley's nonpropositional
justification. Certainly, some elements of logic, such as the law of
noncontradiction and the modus tollens, may be established by your method
of "denying presumes the thing denied", and from such a bare logical
skeleton (for the law of the excluded middle can *not* be established in
this way) most of mathematics can perhaps be derived, but mathematics 
says nothing about induction or the scientific method. And there is 
nothing about induction or the scientific method which we must presume in 
denying their validity.

> >You beg the question that an existence/consciousness
> >dichotomy is real and significant. Until you unbeg this more fundamental
> >question, I cannot interest myself in the question of which half of the
> >false dichotomy has "primacy". 
> 
> What makes you think it's a false dichotomy?

Well, asking a question certainly doesn't unbeg your question, but since
you ask, consciousness is an element of existence. It's a subset, not an
independent and parallel construct. If you want to interpret this as my
agreeing with you about the primacy of existence, you may, but my
agreement has nothing to do with any "axiomatic reasoning". It has to do
with a theory that explains consciousness as an element of existence,
which I accept provisionally in the absence of contradictory evidence or
other effective refutations. This theory does *not* presuppose any
axiomatic grounds; it is posited blindly and groundlessly. I hold it
because it has greater explanatory power, logical strength, and Occamic
simplicity than competing theories, because it is logically consistent,
and because it is not contradicted by any empirical evidence, so far. If I
could come up with an alternative theory that met these criteria, I would
entertain that second theory just as willingly. No such second theory has
suggested itself to date. 

Organisms, even mindless organisms, embody "knowledge" about their
environment in their genes. This knowledge developed by blind variation
among the genes, by selective retention by the environment, and by
accumulation and propagation of the successful variations. The memes that
we call "theories" embody knowledge about our environment in exactly the
same way, and they evolve by exactly the same processes: blind but
fruitful variation, selective retention (by the processes of criticism
which you find so problematic), and cultural propagation. 

> (The primacy of consciousness is self-contradictory because the very concept
> of consciousness presupposes "that which consciousness is of," some existent
> thing that is an object of consciousness.  Consciousness must be conscious-
> ness of something, but it may be consciousness of anything.)

You beg the question that just because consciousness must be of something,
that this something must be necessarily independent of consciousness. 
Just because we find a certain structure in our predicates doesn't
guarantee that the same structure is to be found in the universe. Of
course, I am a realist just as you are, but my reasons for being a realist
are very different from your reasons. 

